      "text": "To determine how many coins are in the stack, we need to consider the thicknesses of the coins and how they add up to a total height of 14 mm. We will use variables to represent the number of each type of coin in the stack and solve for the unknowns.\n\nLet's denote:\n- \\( p \\) as the number of pennies,\n- \\( n \\) as the number of nickels,\n- \\( d \\) as the number of dimes,\n- \\( q \\) as the number of quarters.\n\nThe thickness of each type of coin is given as follows:\n- Penny: 1.55 mm,\n- Nickel: 1.95 mm,\n- Dime: 1.35 mm,\n- Quarter: 1.75 mm.\n\nThe total height of the stack is the sum of the heights of all the coins:\n\\[ 1.55p + 1.95n + 1.35d + 1.75q = 14.00 \\]\n\nTo solve this equation, we need to find integer values for \\( p \\), \\( n \\), \\( d \\), and \\( q \\) that satisfy the equation. We will test different combinations of \\( p \\), \\( n \\), \\( d \\), and \\( q \\) to find a solution.\n\nFirst, let's test the maximum possible value for the number of quarters, which is 8 (since \\( 8 \\times 1.75 = 14.00 \\), the maximum possible sum of all coin heights is \\( 8 \\times 1.75 = 14 \\)).\n\nIf \\( q = 8 \\):\n\\[ 1.75 \\times 8 = 14.00 \\]\nThis is a solution, and we need to check if there are any other combinations of \\( p \\), \\( n \\), and \\( d \\) that work. However, since the total height is exactly 14 mm and the thicknesses must add up to exactly 14 mm, any other combination of \\( p \\), \\( n \\), and \\( d \\) would either exceed 14 mm or not add up to 14 mm.\n\nTo confirm, let's check if there are any other possible values for \\( q \\). If \\( q = 7 \\):\n\\[ 1.75 \\times 7 = 12.25 \\]\nThe remaining height needed is:\n\\[ 14 - 12.25 = 1.75 \\]\nThis is exactly the thickness of one quarter, so \\( d = 1 \\). Now we need to find \\( p \\) and \\( n \\) such that \\( 1.55p + 1.95n = 1.75 \\). Since the maximum height from the pennies and nickels is only 1.70 mm (from 7 pennies and 1 nickel, not enough), it is not possible to have 7 quarters and any number of nickels and pennies that will sum to exactly 14 mm.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is:\n\\[ q = 8, \\quad d = 1, \\quad p = 0, \\quad n = 0. \\]\n\nThe number of coins in the stack is:\n\\[ p + n + d + q = 0 + 0 + 1 + 8 = 9 \\]\n\nThus, the number of coins in the stack is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).",
