      "text": "To determine the number of coins in the stack, we need to consider the maximum number of each type of coin that can fit given the height constraint of 14 mm. We will start by maximizing the number of quarters, then minuscules, and so on until the total height is at least 14 mm.\n\n1. **Calculate the height contribution of each type of coin up to the point where a larger coin would exceed the height limit.**\n\n- **Quarters (Q):**\n  Each quarter is 1.75 mm thick. The maximum number of quarters that can fit is:\n  \\[\n  \\left\\lfloor \\frac{14}{1.75} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 8 \\right\\rfloor = 8\n  \\]\n  The height contribution of 8 quarters is:\n  \\[\n  8 \\times 1.75 = 14 \\text{ mm}\n  \\]\n  Since the height of 8 quarters is exactly 14 mm, we can fit 8 quarters in the stack. The remaining height is:\n  \\[\n  14 - 14 = 0 \\text{ mm}\n  \\]\n  This means we can fit 8 quarters.\n\n2. **Calculate the height contribution of each type of coin up to the point where a larger coin would exceed the height limit.**\n\n- **Nickels (N):**\n  Each nickel is 1.95 mm thick. The maximum number of nickels that can fit is:\n  \\[\n  \\left\\lfloor \\frac{14}{1.95} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 7.19 \\right\\rfloor = 7\n  \\]\n  The height contribution of 7 nickels is:\n  \\[\n  7 \\times 1.95 = 13.65 \\text{ mm}\n  \\]\n  The remaining height is:\n  \\[\n  14 - 13.65 = 0.35 \\text{ mm}\n  \\]\n  Since 13.65 mm is less than 14 mm, we can fit 7 nickels.\n\n- **Dimes (D):**\n  Each dime is 1.35 mm thick. The maximum number of dimes that can fit is:\n  \\[\n  \\left\\lfloor \\frac{14}{1.35} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 10.37 \\right\\rfloor = 10\n  \\]\n  The height contribution of 10 dimes is:\n  \\[\n  10 \\times 1.35 = 13.5 \\text{ mm}\n  \\]\n  The remaining height is:\n  \\[\n  14 - 13.5 = 0.5 \\text{ mm}\n  \\]\n  Since 13.5 mm is less than 14 mm, we can fit 10 dimes.\n\n- **Pennies (P):**\n  Each penny is 1.55 mm thick. The maximum number of pennies that can fit is:\n  \\[\n  \\left\\lfloor \\frac{14}{1.55} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 9.03 \\right\\rfloor = 9\n  \\]\n  The height contribution of 9 pennies is:\n  \\[\n  9 \\times 1.55 = 14.05 \\text{ mm}\n  \\]\n  The remaining height is:\n  \\[\n  14 - 14.05 = -0.05 \\text{ mm}\n  \\]\n  Since the remaining height is negative, we cannot fit any more pennies. However, we need to check the previous fits to ensure we haven't overcounted.\n\nSince we have already established that we can fit 8 quarters, 7 nickels, and 10 dimes, we need to check if any more coins fit with the remaining height.\n\n3. **Check for additional coins:**\n  - **Adding up the current coins:**\n    \\[\n    8 \\text{ quarters} + 7 \\text{ nickels} + 10 \\text{ dimes} = 25 \\text{ coins}\n    \\]\n  - **Remaining height:**\n    \\[\n    14 - 25 \\times 1.55 = -13.75 \\text{ mm} \\quad \\text{(which is negative)}\n    \\]\n    This means we cannot fit any more coins without exceeding the height limit.\n\nTherefore, the total number of coins in the stack is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{25}\n\\]",
